<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Western Liang</h1><p class="st-meta">400 CE – 421 CE · capital Dunhuang, Jiuquan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Chinese-ruled kingdom at Dunhuang, the westernmost of the Sixteen Kingdoms, whose founder Li Gao was later claimed as an ancestor by the Tang emperors.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-gao">Li Gao</a>, a Chinese official of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-clan-of-longxi">Longxi Li</a> family, governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> under <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-liang">Northern Liang</a>. In 400 local officials proclaimed him Duke of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/liang-liang-shidu">Liang</a>, and he broke away to rule the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a>, moving his capital from Dunhuang to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiuquan">Jiuquan</a> in 405.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-gao">Li Gao</a> governed in the Confucian manner, encouraging schools, farming and irrigation, and acknowledged the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin</a> as his sovereign. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a>, at the junction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Roads</a>, was a centre of trade and Buddhist activity, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-liang-555-587">Western Liang</a> preserved Chinese learning on the far frontier.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-gao">Li Gao's</a> son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-xin-western-liang">Li Xin</a> was killed in battle with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-liang">Northern Liang</a> in 420; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juqu-mengxun">Juqu Mengxun</a> then took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiuquan">Jiuquan</a>, and in 421 he flooded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-xun-western-liang">Li Xun</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty's</a> Li emperors traced their ancestry to Li <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao">Gao</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 400 CE – 421 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:67.66%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>400 CE – 421 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dunhuang, Jiuquan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Li Gao, Li Xin, Li Xun</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Confucianism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Western Liang (400-421), Liang (Li Gao)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=410&amp;focus=western-liang" aria-label="Western Liang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7009 742 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Western Liang lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7329 998L7489 1037"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7489 1037h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7329 998h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7329 998h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Dunhuang. <a href="/atlas?year=410&amp;focus=western-liang">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
